'''Infinity 0 — Interview''' is [[The_Storyized_Interviewer]]'s intake interview log for the
''Infinity 0'' input: which output types are needed, and the requirements for each. See
[[Infinity_0]] for the raw input status itself — this page is the *instructions* half of the input wiki
(''SPEC.md'''s "things and instructions"), kept separate since the interview may be revisited later.

== Output types requested ==

* '''LaTeX''' — publisher-ready print PDF. Full print-ready typesetting: trim size '''5.5" x 8.5"'''
  (trade paperback), margins, running heads, ready to send to a printer.
** '''Front/back matter:''' full — title page, table of contents, copyright page, not just story text.
** '''House style:''' generic professional novel layout, not tied to a specific publisher's style guide.
* '''Webpage''' — rich multimedia, linear. Linear story, no branching/choice paths, enhanced with
  images, audio, and animation per scene.
** '''Hosting:''' static site (e.g. GitHub Pages) — no server, static assets only.
** '''Audio:''' AI-generated.
** '''Images:''' AI-generated illustrations.
* '''3D model''' — scene/environment models. Covers places/settings from the book, not characters.
** '''Tech stack:''' TypeScript + WebGL, rendered client-side in a modern browser. Same hosting
   constraint as the webpage output — no server, just static asset hosting.
** '''Scene generation:''' not per-story or per-scene prioritized. When input processing starts, the
   entire input is taken as a whole and used to create a scene from it. (Whether that means one single
   overarching scene for the whole book, or a repeatable process invoked later over sub-units, isn't
   settled yet — see open follow-ups.)
** '''Fidelity:''' concept-level blockout — fast, rough massing/layout, not final-quality.

== Cross-cutting technical constraint ==

Both the '''webpage''' and '''3D model''' outputs are static-only, browser-rendered experiences — no
backend server for either. Both need to be servable from plain static asset hosting. This is a shared
constraint across those two output types, not just a per-type detail.

== Ordering ==

Per `SPEC.md`'s open questions and `Infinity_0`'s own unordered-for-now state: '''each output type
orders independently'''. LaTeX, webpage, and 3D scenes each settle on their own order/structure as
needed — none of them are required to wait for or mirror an eventual order on the input side.

